[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Configure and operate the DMS (/docs/pr-preview/pr-39/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/dms)\n\n\n\nA **Device Management Service (DMS)** configures admission and identity lifecycle for its devices. It acts as a registration authority; the CA selected in `Enrollment CA` signs certificates. This guide is for the policy administrator.\n\nBefore you begin [#before-you-begin]\n\nYou need DMS management permissions, an active CA with a usable key and a tested [issuance profile](/docs/platform/pki/certificate-profiles). Agree device ID, initial credential, consumer and renewal window with the integrator. Review [Provisioning strategies](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/provisioning-strategies) before choosing registration and replacement.\n\nPrepare EST server trust and device connectivity through [Deployment](/docs/deployment/self-hosted/networking). Your operator’s administrative authentication does not replace device EST authentication.\n\nConfigure a DMS [#configure-a-dms]\n\n<Steps>\n  <Step>\n    Identify the fleet [#identify-the-fleet]\n\n    Create the DMS with a recognizable name and retain its ID. EST URLs use this identifier, not the display name. Each device retains one owning DMS.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Select the enrollment CA [#select-the-enrollment-ca]\n\n    Choose **Enrollment CA** and an explicit profile appropriate for the end-entity identity. Keep CN compatible with `Device ID`. The test certificate should allow its intended purpose and expire within issuer validity.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Configure EST [#configure-est]\n\n    Select initial enrollment registration and authentication. Separately configure re-enrollment authentication, thresholds and previous certificate retirement. Leave ServerKeyGen disabled if the client generates its key.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Distribute trust [#distribute-trust]\n\n    Select certificates returned by `/cacerts`. Explicitly include required intermediates and roots, keeping distribution distinct from automatic trust installation.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Verify the endpoints [#verify-the-endpoints]\n\n    Save and read configuration again. Query `/cacerts`, perform an allowed and a rejected enrollment, and inspect device, serial and history. Creating the DMS or downloading cacerts does not establish working authentication and issuance.\n  </Step>\n</Steps>\n\nEnrollment configuration [#enrollment-configuration]\n\nEnrollment Device Registration [#enrollment-device-registration]\n\n`settings.enrollment_settings.registration_mode` accepts `JITP` or `PRE_REGISTRATION`. JITP creates an unknown device with the CSR CN as ID and the DMS as owner. `device_provisioning_profile` supplies tags, metadata and icon for that registration.\n\nPRE\\_REGISTRATION requires an existing ID. In the inspected code, an existing device encounters replacement checks first, even without an identity. Review [preregistration conditions](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/provisioning-strategies#preregistration-and-replacement) before using this mode.\n\nEnrollment Settings [#enrollment-settings]\n\n| `settings.enrollment_settings` field | Decision                                                                                                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `protocol`                           | `EST_RFC7030` for these operations.                                                                                                                        |\n| `enrollment_ca`                      | ID of the signing CA.                                                                                                                                      |\n| `verify_csr_signature`               | Keep `true` to verify CSR key possession.                                                                                                                  |\n| `enable_replaceable_enrollment`      | Allows `/simpleenroll` for an existing ID in the same DMS. It can attempt previous certificate revocation even if a later phase fails; this is not atomic. |\n| `est_rfc7030_settings.auth_mode`     | One of the four authentication modes.                                                                                                                      |\n\nInitial enrollment rejects an ID owned by another DMS. Do not automatically enable replacement in response to an error; [Troubleshooting](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/troubleshooting) distinguishes repeated IDs, ownership and partial changes.\n\nAuthentication per operation [#authentication-per-operation]\n\nThe blocks are independent:\n\n* `settings.enrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings` for `/simpleenroll` and `/serverkeygen`.\n* `settings.reenrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings` for `/simplereenroll`.\n\n| `auth_mode`                               | Control applied in the configured operation          |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE`                      | Presented certificate validation.                    |\n| `EXTERNAL_WEBHOOK`                        | Configured webhook authorization.                    |\n| `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_AND_EXTERNAL_WEBHOOK` | Certificate validation followed by webhook approval. |\n| `NO_AUTH`                                 | No DMS requester authentication.                     |\n\nWebhook and combined modes also execute during re-enrollment when configured in its block. They do not implicitly degrade to NO\\_AUTH. An absent or unrecognized mode fails.\n\nFor initial mTLS, `client_certificate_settings` contains `validation_cas`, `allow_expired` and `chain_level_validation`. The inspected flow receives one certificate credential; it does not guarantee forwarding every handshake intermediate to the validator. For a direct case, configure the bootstrap’s direct issuer as validation CA and test the actual Gateway path.\n\nFor certificate re-enrollment, the validator uses the enrollment CA, then `additional_validation_cas`, and `enable_expired_renewal`. Do not assume copying initial `validation_cas` changes those issuers.\n\nFor a webhook, configure destination, Lamassu-to-service authentication, method and timeout. The [authentication contract](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/bootstrap-identity) explains the device credential and request body. `NO_AUTH` does not establish ownership of the requested ID.\n\nExternal webhook settings [#external-webhook-settings]\n\nThe webhook authentication modes read `external_webhook_settings` in the same `est_rfc7030_settings` block:\n\n| `external_webhook_settings` field | Decision                                                                                                                                     |\n| --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `url`                             | Absolute `http` or `https` URL of the receiver. It must be the final URL; redirects are not followed.                                        |\n| `method`                          | `POST` or `PUT`; `POST` by default.                                                                                                          |\n| `name`                            | Label used in service logs.                                                                                                                  |\n| `config.auth_mode`                | `noauth`, `apikey`, `jwt` or `mtls`. An absent or unknown value behaves as `noauth`.                                                         |\n| `config.log_level`                | Log level of the webhook call: `debug`, `info`, `warn` or `error`; `info` by default.                                                        |\n| `config.apikey_options`           | Required for `apikey`: `header` and `key`. Reserved header names are rejected.                                                               |\n| `config.jwt_options`              | Required for `jwt`: `oidc_client_id`, `oidc_client_secret` and an absolute `http` or `https` `oidc_well_known`.                              |\n| `config.mtls_options`             | Required for `mtls`: `cert_content` and `key_content` as inline PEM.                                                                         |\n| `config.insecure_skip_verify`     | Skips verification of the receiver certificate when `true`. Verification is on by default. Unlike alert channels, this flag is honored here. |\n| `config.call_timeout`             | Maximum duration of the authorization call (for example `10s`); 10 seconds by default.                                                       |\n\nRules for that configuration:\n\n* Creating or updating a DMS whose enrollment or reenrollment `auth_mode` uses the webhook validates the block and returns HTTP 400 — the body starts with `struct validation error:` and continues with `invalid enrollment external webhook config` or `invalid reenrollment external webhook config` — when the URL, method or options are invalid. File references (`ca_cert_file`, `mtls_options.cert_file`, `mtls_options.key_file`) are always invalid: the service does not read files, so supply the PEM as `mtls_options.cert_content` and `key_content`.\n* A custom `apikey_options.header` may not use names that the platform or the receiving service trusts — for example `X-Principal-Id`, `X-Request-Id`, `Host`, `Content-Type` or `Forwarded` — and no name may start with `X-Forwarded-`, `X-Envoy-` or `X-Lms-` (case-insensitive). The [DMS API reference](/docs/api-reference/dms-manager) documents the `WebhookCall` and `WebhookCallHttpClient` schemas and that reserved API-key headers are rejected.\n* The nested `http_client` key exists only in alert channel configurations; in a DMS body it is ignored. The DMS key is `external_webhook_settings.config`.\n* The API ignores the legacy fields `validate_server_cert`, `apikey`, `oidc` and `mtls`. A body with `config.auth_mode` set to `apikey`, `jwt` or `mtls` that still carries only the legacy option fields (`apikey`, `oidc`, `mtls`) fails validation with 400; only legacy `noauth` bodies are accepted, and their credentials are dropped.\n* The authorization response must be JSON of at most 1 MiB; a larger body fails the call.\n\n<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"Credentials over http://\">\n  With credentials configured, they are sent regardless of the URL scheme: an `http://` webhook receives the API key or bearer token in cleartext, and mTLS has no effect because no TLS handshake takes place, so the client certificate is never presented. Prefer `https://` for `url`, `oidc_well_known` and the token endpoint it advertises.\n</Callout>\n\nMigration `20260604110000` copies initial authentication into renewal settings if missing and the required structure exists. It preserves existing configuration. After upgrading, read both blocks<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">; if your version’s console does not expose independent settings, use the </del>[<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">DMS API</del>](/docs/api-reference/dms-manager)<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">. The console exposes independent editors for the enrollment and re-enrollment authentication, with the same Log Level, Call Timeout, HTTP Authentication and credential fields as the webhook channels, plus a </ins>**<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">Skip TLS Verification</ins>**<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\"> switch (off by default, so verification stays on; the console warns when you enable it)</ins>.\n\nMigration `20261021120000` rewrites every stored webhook config into this layout: `validate_server_cert` becomes the negated `insecure_skip_verify` (a config where `validate_server_cert` was false keeps skipping verification), `oidc` becomes `jwt_options`, `apikey` becomes `apikey_options` and `mtls` becomes `mtls_options`. When both the legacy certificate and key are PEM, they move into `mtls_options.cert_content` and `key_content` and need no action. Otherwise, including a mixed config (PEM certificate, file-path key), both values become `mtls_options.cert_file` and `key_file`. Delivery rejects any webhook config that references files, whatever its auth mode, so every enroll and reenroll that calls that webhook fails until the certificate and key are re-entered as PEM content, or the leftover file fields are removed if the webhook does not use mTLS. <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">If your version's console does not expose those fields, edit the DMS through the </del>[<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">DMS API</del>](/docs/api-reference/dms-manager)<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">The console now exposes these fields: open the DMS, replace any file-path value with PEM content — the editor rejects file paths with an inline field error — and save. Saving rewrites `external_webhook_settings.config` with the current keys, and the console reads both plain and base64-encoded PEM from migrated configurations and stores the material base64-encoded</ins>.\n\nIssuance profile [#issuance-profile]\n\n`settings.issuance_profile_id` takes precedence over `settings.issuance_profile`; without either, the CA default profile is used. Rules are documented once in [Profiles](/docs/platform/pki/certificate-profiles). DMS changes affect future requests; they do not modify signed certificates or install client trust.\n\nRe-enrollment configuration [#re-enrollment-configuration]\n\n| `settings.reenrollment_settings` field | Purpose                                                                                                                                          |\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `reenrollment_delta`                   | Opens the window at the active certificate’s `NotAfter` minus this delta.                                                                        |\n| `preventive_delta`                     | Preventive operational monitoring threshold.                                                                                                     |\n| `critical_delta`                       | Critical operational monitoring threshold.                                                                                                       |\n| `enable_expired_renewal`               | Accepts an expired presented certificate in the renewal mTLS validator. Does not authorize another mode or remove Gateway constraints by itself. |\n| `additional_validation_cas`            | Additional mTLS credential issuers during migration.                                                                                             |\n| `revoke_on_reenrollment`               | If the previous certificate is ACTIVE, attempts Superseded revocation before binding its successor.                                              |\n\nMonitoring does not renew: the client must schedule the request and installation. Webhook and NO\\_AUTH modes do not use the same mTLS expired-credential validator; the webhook must explicitly authorize permitted recovery. Review [Renewal and recovery](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/renewal-and-recovery) for operation order and partial failures.\n\nServerKeyGen [#serverkeygen]\n\n`settings.server_keygen_settings.enabled` enables software key generation on the server. Algorithm and size come from its `key` field; the profile must permit them. The response contains an unencrypted PKCS#8 key inside TLS alongside the certificate. See format and extraction in [EST reference](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/est-reference#serverkeygen).\n\nTrust distribution [#trust-distribution]\n\n| Option                        | Published material                                                         |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Include Lamassu System CA** | Available downstream TLS certificate; it can be a leaf, not an issuing CA. |\n| **Include Enrollment CA**     | Selected issuing CA certificate.                                           |\n| **Managed CAs**               | Explicitly selected CA certificates.                                       |\n\nAll ancestors are not added automatically. [Trust distribution](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/trust-distribution) distinguishes trusted roots, intermediates and initial server trust.\n\nOperate the DMS inventory [#operate-the-dms-inventory]\n\n**Edit** changes name, settings and metadata. Backend updates replace these complete blocks; read the DMS before sending API edits to avoid losing fields.\n\n**Go to DMS owned devices** restricts inventory by owner. **Show/Edit Metadata** contains operational and integration data; consult the integration guide before changing it. **EST (RFC-7030)** helps locate URLs.\n\n**Delete** removes the DMS record and configuration; it does not itself cascade revocation across every device and certificate. Migrate dependencies and retain audit references before retirement.\n\nIntegrate the devices [#integrate-the-devices]\n\nContinue with [EST client](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/est-client) or the [enrollment and mTLS tutorial](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/quickstarts/enroll-device). [EST reference](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/est-reference) covers formats; [Troubleshooting](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/troubleshooting) covers failures.\n"]
